1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,560
We have been talking about heaps,
collectors, and dumps as if every new in
source becomes a heap object GC must
eventually judge. That model is useful —
and incomplete.

2
00:00:10,679 --> 00:00:24,119
After the JIT warms up, the runtime may
prove that some objects never escape the
method that created them. If nothing
outside needs to see that object as an
object, the JIT can optimize it away.

3
00:00:24,239 --> 00:00:37,159
That is escape analysis — one reason
microbenchmarks lie, and why "I allocated
less in source" does not always match
profiles. Here is a tiny example that
forces the question: Look at this java for
a moment.

4
00:00:37,280 --> 00:00:40,079
In source, you clearly constructed a
Point.

5
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:51,920
In a hot method, after inlining and escape
analysis, the JIT may scalar-replace it:
keep 1 and 2 in registers or stack slots,
never allocate a heap Point, and still
return the sum.

6
00:00:52,040 --> 00:01:04,280
From the programmer's view there was an
object. From the machine's view there may
only have been values. If an allocation
profile wonders where Point went, it may
never have become a heap citizen on this
path.

7
00:01:04,400 --> 00:01:05,920
What does "escape" mean?

8
00:01:06,100 --> 00:01:18,140
If the object is returned to an unknown
caller, stored in a field, placed into a
collection that outlives the method,
published to another thread, or otherwise
made reachable outside the analyzing
scope, it escapes.

9
00:01:18,260 --> 00:01:28,540
If it stays local and the JIT can see all
uses, it may not. Non-escaping objects
enable scalar replacement — breaking an
object into its fields.

10
00:01:28,659 --> 00:01:43,060
Sometimes more: if you synchronize on an
object the JIT proves is thread-local and
never escapes, lock elision becomes
possible. The source still shows
synchronized; optimized native code may
not pay for a real monitor.

11
00:01:43,179 --> 00:01:53,579
That is not a license to sprinkle
synchronized blocks randomly. It explains
why some synchronized local objects do not
show up as contended locks. Walk the Point
variations.

12
00:01:53,699 --> 00:02:05,539
Local carrier that never leaves the method
— scalar replacement may apply. Store p
into a list that outlives the method — it
escapes. Return p — it escapes.

13
00:02:05,659 --> 00:02:12,459
Pass p into a method the JIT cannot inline
or analyze fully — escape may be assumed
conservatively.

14
00:02:12,579 --> 00:02:21,659
The keyword is not "never allocate." It is
"allocate when the object must be visible
as an object." This is exciting in
interviews and dangerous in reviews.

15
00:02:21,779 --> 00:02:34,259
Do not rewrite clear code into contortions
because you imagine every new is
expensive. Readability still wins off the
proven hot path; the JIT may already
remove the allocation you are about to
uglify.

16
00:02:34,379 --> 00:02:43,379
Do not trust a tiny microbenchmark without
JMH and a warmup story that matches
production. Escape analysis depends on
inlining and profiles.

17
00:02:43,499 --> 00:02:52,579
A cold microbench can miss the
optimization or panic about allocations
that disappear in the real service.
Connect this to GC pressure conversations.

18
00:02:52,699 --> 00:03:02,819
A developer deletes a temporary object in
a hot loop and declares victory. Sometimes
profiles improve. Sometimes nothing
changes because the object never escaped
after warmup.

19
00:03:02,939 --> 00:03:14,059
Sometimes things get worse because the
"optimization" blocked inlining. Escape
analysis does not forbid caring about
allocations. It forbids caring about them
without profiles from the warmed path.

20
00:03:14,179 --> 00:03:27,059
Lock elision deserves one grounded
picture. You synchronize on a freshly
created local lock object "to be safe." No
other thread can see it. After JIT
optimization, the monitor may disappear.

21
00:03:27,179 --> 00:03:36,419
The review should still ask why the lock
exists for readers — but the performance
conversation should not assume contention
the runtime proved impossible. So when do
you care?

22
00:03:36,599 --> 00:03:45,799
When allocation profiles under real load
show pressure. When synchronized blocks on
obviously local objects make you wonder
whether contention is imaginary.

23
00:03:45,919 --> 00:03:52,239
When someone claims "Java always allocates
this" as a reason to rewrite an API into
primitive soup before measuring.

24
00:03:52,359 --> 00:04:00,759
Measure with real workloads: JFR
allocation samples and async-profiler show
what survived into the heap, not what your
eyes saw in source.

25
00:04:00,879 --> 00:04:14,399
If asked what escape analysis is, say: the
JIT analyzes whether an object escapes a
method or thread; if it does not,
optimizations like scalar replacement and
sometimes lock elision become legal.

26
00:04:14,519 --> 00:04:22,359
Then add humility: do not micro-optimize
assuming every new escapes, and do not
rewrite clear code for imaginary
allocations.

27
00:04:22,479 --> 00:04:31,639
Today's lesson connects the JIT back to
the heap: not every object you construct
becomes collector work. Next we leave the
"objects on the heap" frame.

28
00:04:31,759 --> 00:04:40,799
Processes die with free heap space when
native memory, metaspace, or container
limits are the real budget — and that
paradox needs its own explanation.
